{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' .planning/log/2026-08.md\"","aggregated_output":"# August 2026\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Add trust-tier policy plumbing\n\n`working tree` · area: module provenance and activation policy\n\nLocks now retain a typed provenance tier and optional detached-signature\nmetadata. Root graphs may set `trustPolicy.minimum`; graph construction blocks\nlower-tier modules before dependency/provider/frontend activation, while CLI\nand shell installs refuse candidates below that policy and staged update review\nsurfaces trust-policy diagnostics even when revisions are unchanged. Existing\nworkspaces remain permissive by default. Cryptographic signature verification\nand registry key distribution remain open follow-up work.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\nfocused trust-policy regression could not start because `sha2` is absent from\nthe local registry cache; offline execution reports the same missing\ndependency.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Isolate frontend source indexing failures\n\n`working tree` · area: frontend component catalog\n\nFrontend catalog construction now stays separate from the resolved module\ngraph. Primary `.mesh` roots and extension-point contributions compile as\nindependent incremental entries, unchanged entries are reused by manifest and\nwatched-source fingerprints, and a failed module or contribution is retained\nas a scoped catalog diagnostic instead of aborting unrelated entries. Each\ndeclared contribution source remains in the host watch set so an edit can\nretry a previously invalid source without changing graph activation state.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. The\nfocused catalog regression could not start because `sha2` is absent from the\nlocal registry cache; offline execution reports the same missing dependency.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Explain candidate graph effects during dry runs\n\n`working tree` · area: package planning and CLI review\n\nResolved module graphs now expose a deterministic diff for additions, removals,\nupdates, enable/disable transitions, provider changes, and effective profile\nlayout/slot changes. Update planning compares the installed graph with the\nfully staged candidate graph, and the CLI prints that diff alongside existing\ngrant, contract-break, and dependency diagnostics before the transaction can\ncommit. `--dry-run` therefore reviews the same normalized graph the runtime will\nactivate without changing live state.\n\nVerification: formatting and diff checks pass. The focused graph-diff test\ncould not start because `sha2` is absent from the local registry cache and\nnetwork access is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Make the resolved graph the runtime inventory\n\n`working tree` · area: module graph and shell discovery\n\nShell discovery now loads one validated `InstalledModuleGraph` candidate and\nconstructs runtime module instances, provider registrations, settings schemas,\nand frontend catalog inputs from that graph. It no longer discovers arbitrary\nmodule manifests and treats a missing graph as an allow-all filter. Graph load\nfailure retains the last-known-good runtime; startup with no valid graph\nactivates no module. Duplicate dependency validation in the shell was removed\nso the package graph is the normalized compatibility authority.\n\nVerification: formatting and diff checks pass. Cargo tests remain unavailable\nbecause the local registry cache lacks `sha2` and network access is disabled.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Publish immutable module activation generations\n\n`working tree` · area: module store and graph activation\n\nPackage install, update, uninstall, and rollback now publish validated module\ntrees into a shared SHA-256 content-addressed store. Objects and generation\nsnapshots are read-only after atomic publication; the lock generation activates\nonly after the matching snapshot exists. Installed-graph and shell discovery\nconsume the active immutable object paths when available, while editable module\ntrees remain the authoring and local-edit source. Package transaction recovery\nprotects the active-generation pointer.\n\nVerification: `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` pass. Focused\nCargo tests could not start because `sha2` is absent from the local registry\ncache and network access to crates.io is unavailable.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Recover saved Wi-Fi on Codex network waits\n\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\n\nThe backlog runner now reacts to live Codex `waiting for network` errors when\nNetworkManager is available: it enables Wi-Fi and attempts the most recently\nused saved wireless profile, throttled to one attempt per 30 seconds. Recovery\nis best-effort, does not handle credentials, and can be disabled with\n`CODEX_WIFI_RECOVERY=0`.\n\n## 2026-08-21 — Fall back from stale temporary-directory settings\n\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\n\nThe backlog runner now skips a missing or unwritable `TMPDIR` and falls back to\n`/tmp`, which keeps it usable after a Nix shell's temporary directory has been\nremoved. It reports the fallback and fails with an explicit diagnostic only\nwhen neither location can create the capture directory.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Fail closed graph candidates and retain live activation\n\n`working tree` · area: module graph activation and shell runtime reloads\n\nGraph-backed frontend catalogs now compile and mount only enabled modules from\nthe validated installed graph. Discovery no longer registers module-owned\ncontracts or providers before graph validation, invalid profile roots fail\nclosed during startup, and graph replacement uses a prepare/commit boundary so\nfailed reloads keep the last-known-good graph and active frontend/backend\nruntime. Package removal also validates the replacement graph before removing\nthe in-memory module or deactivating its live surface.\n\nVerification: the new last-known-good graph reload regression passed; focused\ncatalog and frontend activation tests passed; `cargo check -p mesh-core-shell`\nand formatting checks passed. The full shell library ran 674 passed, 6 failed,\nand 130 ignored; the six failures are the known hover, raster, inspector,\nbackend-manifest, debug-layout-label, and theme-contract baseline failures\nrecorded by the Section 15 audit. No performance claim is made.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Recover from non-object settings documents\n\nSection 01 foundation hardening is implemented. `SettingsStore::load_from` and\n`SettingsStore::from_value` now turn successfully parsed non-object documents\ninto an empty usable store with declared shell defaults, while retaining one\nerror diagnostic at the document root. I/O and irrecoverable JSON parse errors\nremain fatal.\n\nVerification: `mesh-core-config --lib` passed 35 tests;\n`mesh-core-surface-config --lib` passed 28 tests; `cargo check --workspace`\npassed with existing warnings. No performance claim is made.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Use current context usage for runner rollover\n\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\n\nThe Codex `--json` turn usage is cumulative session accounting, so treating it\nas current context occupancy produced impossible negative remaining percentages\nafter long turns. The runner now reads the latest non-ephemeral rollout's\n`last_token_usage.total_tokens` and `model_context_window`, honors an explicit\n`CODEX_CONTEXT_WINDOW_TOKENS` override, and clamps the displayed percentage.\nWhen the rollout signal is unavailable, it reports the limitation and starts a\nfresh session conservatively instead of presenting a false context estimate.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Add explicit interrupted-turn recovery\n\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\n\nThe backlog runner keeps its clean-tree guard as the default but now accepts\n`--allow-dirty` for an explicitly requested retry after a canceled turn left\nthe feature edits in place. The option reports the existing status and allows\nthe next bounded turn to finish and commit that work; normal invocations still\nrefuse to risk incorporating unrelated changes.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Stream concise Codex runner progress\n\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\n\nThe backlog runner now tees Codex JSONL into its capture file while filtering\nlive output to concise session, turn, command, error, and completion updates.\nLarge command `aggregated_output` and agent-message bodies are retained for\npost-turn parsing but are no longer dumped to the terminal when a run is\ncancelled or completes.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Fix unattended Codex permission mode\n\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\n\nThe backlog runner now defaults to Codex's explicit unrestricted execution flag\nfor unattended turns. The previous `--sandbox workspace-write` plus\n`--approve-for-me` combination is rejected by the installed CLI; the runner now\nuses `--dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox`, with `CODEX_ALLOW_ALL=0` as a\nworkspace-write fallback and no incompatible flag combination.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Add guarded Codex backlog runner\n\n`working tree` · area: developer automation\n\nAdded `scripts/codex-backlog-runner.sh` and its output schema. The runner\nselects the next unchecked backlog item, gives Codex one bounded feature turn,\nrequires the item’s removal and planning log record, and refuses to continue\nunless exactly one non-merge commit was created directly on `main`. It resumes\nthe same Codex session between features while its reported usage remains above\nthe configured rollover threshold, then starts a clean session at approximately\n30% context remaining. The context window defaults to 1,050,000 tokens for the\nGPT-5.6 family and is configurable for other models. No production MESH code\nchanged.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Add feature-level section audit checklist\n\nThe 16 section `improvements.md` audits are now represented in the canonical\n[`docs/BACKLOG.md`](../../docs/BACKLOG.md) as feature-level unchecked items,\ngrouped by section. The checklist includes each audit finding and explicit\nfeature-direction item; shipped Section 01 findings and audit regression\nmatrices remain recorded in their existing log/status documents. No production\ncode changed and no performance claim is made.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Version debug telemetry DTOs and close parity gaps\n\nSection 01 improvement 6 is implemented. Public debug inspection and profiling\nDTOs now derive serde serialization; profiling stage and repaint enum labels\nretain their established wire names, and the `mesh.debug` payload carries\n`DEBUG_TELEMETRY_SCHEMA_VERSION`. Narrow invalidation fields (`narrow_path`,\n`affected_node_count`, and `component.script_narrow`) are serialized and covered\nby a shell regression test. Overlay/controller fields remain explicitly\nhost-owned, while the module graph keeps its established nested wire shape via\na compatibility adapter.\n\nVerification: `mesh-core-debug --lib` passed 2 tests; shell benchmark filtered\ntests passed 16 with 26 ignored; profiling filtered tests passed 26; and debug\nfiltered tests passed 24 with 2 existing dirty-tree failures. No performance\nclaim is made.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Remove the unused foundation EventBus\n\nSection 01 improvement 5 is implemented by removing the unused\n`mesh-core-events` workspace crate, shell field, and orphaned dependencies.\nThe live system already routes extension publications through the scripting\nqueue and typed shell/service router; no production caller used the foundation\nbus. This removes the contract whose unknown-channel and send-failure errors\nwere unreachable and whose payloads were not actually typed.\n\nVerification: `cargo check -p mesh-core-shell -p mesh-core-scripting` passed\nunder `nix develop`; the lockfile contains no `mesh-core-events` package. No\nperformance claim is made.\n\n## 2026-08-20 — Key diagnostics by module instance and issue lifecycle\n\nSection 01 improvement 4 is implemented. Diagnostics now keep stable issue\nrecords keyed by module, instance, and issue code with severity, current\ndetails, first/last seen timestamps, occurrence counts, and active/resolved\nstate. Health is derived from active issues, so warnings cannot downgrade an\nactive error and `healthy()` cannot hide unrelated faults. Collector\nregistrations replace the same identity, explicit unregister removes stale\nprofile/component state, and module snapshots aggregate deterministically while\nretaining instance detail. Backend `Running` transitions resolve prior\nlifecycle issues.\n\nVerification: `mesh-core-diagnostics --lib` passed 6 tests; shell service\ncontract tests passed 12; backend-lifecycle tests passed 22 with 1 existing\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
